<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leonine Wall</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">848–852</span> · Also known as Leonine City</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Leonine Wall is a defensive wall built around the Vatican hill and Saint Peter's Basilica by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-iv">Pope Leo IV</a> between 848 and 852, after Muslim raiders sacked the basilica in 846. The enclosed district became known as the Leonine City. Parts of the wall survive within Vatican City, including the fortified corridor later built on it to link the Vatican with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-santangelo">Castel Sant'Angelo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leonine-wall.jpg" alt="Leonine Wall" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plan_of_Rome_in_the_Middle_Ages_-_Historical_Atlas_by_William_R._Shepherd,_1923.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
